[ ] Build a WASM binding host over vault-style delivery $A browser/editor host supplies inventories and snapshots, executes the same EffectBatch against a vault API, and reuses Core codecs with zero format logic outside Core. Represent weaker atomicity, conflict, and partial failure honestly.$ !3 +wasm ^2026-08-20T09:41 #01a0200c-94ed-7152-acb3-1e173fedda40
[ ] Merge workspace-fs and cli into one effectful runtime crate, one binary $DECIDED 2026-09-18 with the user. Two crates: clearhead-core stays pure and separate (the WASM constraint is the only load-bearing boundary), and one effectful crate absorbs clearhead-workspace-fs plus the CLI commands, sparql and dataset, shipping the single clearhead binary. workspace-fs was only split out to serve multiple binaries. Orthogonality is kept as one-directional module dependencies and pub(crate) visibility, not crate count, with a small test that checks frontends never import each other. This removes the bin-private mod commands blocker at the root. Unblocks the mcp-scaffold action: clearhead mcp becomes a subcommand behind an mcp feature that implies sparql, with build/run split so nothing but the protocol writes to stdout. The LSP folds into the same binary later as its own action, since it carries a migration for the nvim plugin and an unsaved-buffer versus on-disk question for sparql. Not decided: how an id-less charter is represented without a clock in core.$ !2 ^2026-09-18T10:51 #01a0b5a4-ec3f-76ad-a63a-ca655a83364c
[ ] Fold the LSP into the single clearhead binary as a subcommand $Retire the second binary. The crate merge moves the LSP source into the one effectful crate as a module and keeps clearhead-lsp as a second bin target so the nvim plugin cmd does not break; this action removes that target in favour of the clearhead lsp subcommand and migrates the nvim cmd. Costs to plan for: installed setups must change their LSP cmd, and sparql reads the dataset from disk, so a query answered inside the LSP sees saved files rather than unsaved buffers. Decide whether that is acceptable or whether LSP-side queries are limited to cases where it does not matter. Sequenced after the merge; nothing blocks on it. See docs/crate-merge-and-charter-identity.md step 6.$ !5 ^2026-09-18T11:03 #01a0b5b0-17df-74a1-ba34-bd9d04ac34a7
[ ] Ratchet Core's remaining clock and RNG sites toward zero $Follow-up from the I1 audit, 2026-09-18 (scripts/pure-core-allowlist.txt in clearhead-core, branch gate-scripts). The plan expected two sites; the audit found nine in seven files. The identity split removes the two charter sites (workspace/charter.rs and workspace/store/load.rs). The rest, each a separate small change: the actions parser mints an id for an id-less action on every read (parse_action_recursive, workspace/actions/parser.rs); domain constructors Charter new, charter_from_plans_and_name and a Default impl mint ids (domain/mod.rs); Local now in archive_actions.rs (plan_action_archive is a thin wrapper over the already pure plan_action_archive_at, so only the wrapper moves), domain/update.rs apply_updates, and the future-date lint in workspace/actions/lint.rs; telemetry.rs stamps its own event times, so first decide whether telemetry belongs in Core at all. The gate requires the allowlist to match the source exactly, so each removal must tighten the list in the same diff.$ !3 ^2026-09-18T22:05 #01a0b80e-71bd-7059-8842-88113c359ae8
